==English==
{{wikipedia|Quorn (disambiguation)}}

===Etymology===
The fox hunt, the meat substitute<ref>''Oxford Dictionary of English'' (2010), page 1459</ref> and the town in Australia are named after the village in Leicestershire.

===Pronunciation===
*: {{rhymes|ɔː(r)n|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-uk-Quorn.ogg|Audio (UK)|lang=en}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{lb|en|with "the"}} A famous [[fox hunt]] (one of the world's oldest, established in 1696) in [[Leicestershire]].
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Offing|chapter=XII|passage=I've never hunted myself, but I understand that half the battle is being able to make noises like some jungle animal with dyspepsia, and I believe that Aunt Dahlia in her prime could lift fellow-members of the '''Quorn''' and Pytchley out of their saddles with a single yip, though separated from them by two ploughed fields and a spinney.}}
# A village (formerly ''[[Quorndon]]'') in [[Leicestershire]], [[England]], UK.
# A town in [[South Australia]].
# A [[mycoprotein]]-based [[food]] [[product]] used as a [[substitute]] for [[meat]].
